%S A399658 2,6,9,18,30,42,46,62,90,96,121,152,174,210,215,240,284,342,354,398,
%T A399658 434,474,529,573,605,702,722,770,870,930,936,972,1122,1144,1225,1269,
%U A399658 1352,1432,1521,1614,1722,1730,1834,1901,1970,2062,2198,2300,2382,2550,2601,2664,2862,2898,3006,3106
%N A399658 Number of jumps until the n-th return to 0 of the walk on the integers that starts at 0 and, on its j-th visit to an integer x, jumps to x - j if x > 0 and to x + j if x <= 0.
%C A399658 The starting position counts as the first visit to 0, but not as a return. Each jump goes j units toward 0, or to the right from 0; it may pass over 0, and only landing on 0 counts as a return.
%C A399658 The walk returns to 0 infinitely often, and before the n-th return it stays in [-n+1, n].
%C A399658 a(n) <= n*(n+1), with equality if and only if 2n+1 is a prime with primitive root 2 (A001122), i.e., n = 1 or n is in A163782. Hence a(n) = n*(n+1) for infinitely many n if and only if A001122 is infinite, which is the base-2 case of Artin's conjecture on primitive roots.
%C A399658 The statements in the two preceding comments and the formulas, except the conjecture, are proved in the Proofs link.
%C A399658 For n <= 100000, the minimum of (a(n)-n^2)/n is -27733/8192 = -3.3853759765625, attained only at n = 16384, where a(n) = 268379990.
%C A399658 A358338 gives the positions of the analogous walk on the nonnegative integers, which on its j-th visit to x moves to |x - j|.
%C A399658 A401747 gives the corresponding numbers for the walk on the nonnegative integers that starts at 0 and, on its j-th visit to x, jumps to x - j if j <= x and to j - x - 1 if j > x. A401747(n) <= n*(n+1)/2, with equality if and only if n is in A054639.
%H A399658 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A399658/b399658.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399658 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A399658/a399658.png">Plot of (a(n)-n^2)/n for n = 1..100000</a>.
%H A399658 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A399658/a399658.txt">Proofs of the bounds and of the primitive root criterion</a>.
%H A399658 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A399658/a399658_1.txt">Rust program</a>.
%F A399658 n + floor((n+1)^2/4) <= a(n) <= n^2 - n + A002326(n) <= n*(n+1), where A002326(n) is the multiplicative order of 2 modulo 2n+1.
%F A399658 If 2n+1 is in A001122, then a(n+1) = n*(n+1) + A002326(n+1).
%F A399658 Conjecture: a(n) >= n^2 - 5*n (verified for n <= 100000).
%e A399658 The first visit to 0 gives a jump of length 1 to 1. The first visit to 1 gives a jump of length 1 back to 0, so a(1) = 2.
%e A399658 Next, the second visit to 0 gives a jump of length 2 to 2. The first visit to 2 gives a jump of length 1 to 1. The second visit to 1 gives a jump of length 2 to -1, passing over 0 without a return. The first visit to -1 gives a jump of length 1 to 0. Thus the positions through the second return are 0, 1, 0, 2, 1, -1, 0, and a(2) = 6.
%e A399658 Then the third visit to 0 gives a jump of length 3 to 3, the first visit to 3 gives a jump of length 1 to 2, and the second visit to 2 gives a jump of length 2 to 0, so a(3) = 9. Here a(3) < 3*4 because 2 is not a primitive root modulo the prime 7, as 2^3 == 1 (mod 7); indeed a(3) = 3^2 - 3 + A002326(3) = 9.
%o A399658 (Rust) // See Ferudun link.
%Y A399658 Cf. A001122, A002326, A054639, A163782, A358338, A401747.
